[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Micro Frontends with Angular: A Technical Tutorial"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Micro frontends represent an architectural style where a frontend app is decomposed into smaller, independently deployable units. Each micro frontend is owned by a separate team, fostering autonomy and faster development cycles. This tutorial explores how to implement micro frontends using Angular."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Core Concepts"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Independent Deployability: Each micro frontend can be deployed without affecting other parts of the application."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Technology Agnostic: Different micro frontends can be built using different technologies (though consistency is generally preferred)."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Autonomous Teams: Each micro frontend is owned by a separate team, allowing for independent development and deployment."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementation Strategies"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Several strategies exist for composing micro frontends. We'll focus on using Web Components and Module Federation."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Web Components Approach"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Web Components allow you to create reusable custom HTML elements. Angular Elements can be used to package Angular components as Web Components."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"ng add @angular/elements"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"This command adds the necessary packages to your Angular project. Next, you'll need to create an Angular component and convert it into a Web Component."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"import { createCustomElement } from '@angular/elements';
import { Component, Injector } from '@angular/core';
import { BrowserModule } from '@angular/platform-browser';

@Component({
  selector: 'my-element',
  template: '<span>Hello from my element!</span>'
})
export class MyElementComponent {}

export class AppModule {
  constructor(private injector: Injector) {}

  ngDoBootstrap() {
    const el = createCustomElement(MyElementComponent, { injector: this.injector });
    customElements.define('my-element', el);
  }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"In your AppModule, use the 'createCustomElement' function to convert your component into a custom element. Then, define the custom element using 'customElements.define()'."},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Finally, build the Angular project. The resulting JavaScript file can be included in any HTML page, and the custom element '<my-element>' can be used."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Module Federation Approach"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Module Federation, available in Webpack 5, allows you to dynamically load parts of another application at runtime. This is a powerful technique for building micro frontends."},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"First, configure each Angular application as a 'remote' or 'host'. A 'remote' exposes modules, while a 'host' consumes them."},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Install '@angular-architects/module-federation' to simplify the setup:"},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"npm install @angular-architects/module-federation --save-dev"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"Use the 'ng add' schematic to configure your applications:"},{"order":22,"type":"code","text":"ng add @angular-architects/module-federation --project app1 --port 4201 --type remote
ng add @angular-architects/module-federation --project app2 --port 4200 --type host"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"This command configures 'app1' as a remote on port 4201 and 'app2' as a host on port 4200. Adjust the project names and ports accordingly."},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"Expose a module in the remote application (app1):"},{"order":25,"type":"code","text":"// webpack.config.js (in app1)
module.exports = {
  // ... other configurations
  plugins: [
    new ModuleFederationPlugin({
      name: 'app1',
      filename: 'remoteEntry.js',
      exposes: {
        './Module': './src/app/my-module/my-module.module.ts',
      },
      shared: {
        '@angular/core': { eager: true, singleton: true },
        '@angular/common': { eager: true, singleton: true },
        '@angular/router': { eager: true, singleton: true },
      },
    }),
  ],
};"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"Consume the remote module in the host application (app2):"},{"order":27,"type":"code","text":"// webpack.config.js (in app2)
module.exports = {
  // ... other configurations
  plugins: [
    new ModuleFederationPlugin({
      name: 'app2',
      remotes: {
        'app1': 'app1@http://localhost:4201/remoteEntry.js',
      },
      shared: {
        '@angular/core': { eager: true, singleton: true },
        '@angular/common': { eager: true, singleton: true },
        '@angular/router': { eager: true, singleton: true },
      },
    }),
  ],
};"},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the host application, you can now dynamically load the 'Module' from 'app1' using dynamic imports or routing configurations."},{"order":29,"type":"subheading","text":"Routing Considerations"},{"order":30,"type":"paragraph","text":"When using micro frontends, consider how routing will be handled. Options include:"},{"order":31,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Client-Side Routing: The host application handles all routing and loads micro frontends as needed."},{"order":32,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Server-Side Routing: A reverse proxy directs traffic to the appropriate micro frontend based on the URL."},{"order":33,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":34,"type":"paragraph","text":"Micro frontends offer significant benefits in terms of team autonomy and independent deployability. Angular provides powerful tools like Angular Elements and Module Federation to implement this architecture effectively. Choose the approach that best suits your project's needs and team structure."}]
